Despite knowing that the iPhone Duo would face "severe supply constraints" due to manufacturing challenges, Apple chose to begin iPhone Duo pre-orders <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/16/iphone-duo-now-available-to-pre-order/">on Friday</a> instead of waiting until it had more inventory ready, according to <em>Bloomberg</em>'s Mark Gurman. In his <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-10-18/apple-homeview-with-robotic-arm-new-mac-ipad-mini-nov-6-release-iphone-duo"><em>Power On</em> newsletter today</a>, he called this decision a "major mistake."<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/iphone-duo-feature.jpg" alt="" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1068262" /><br/>
"Let me cut to the chase: Apple made a major mistake here," he said. "The company knew there would be severe supply constraints but went ahead with the launch anyway, apparently prioritizing a desire to get the device on the market early in its fiscal first quarter over satisfying demand. That will disappoint many of the early adopters most eager to buy it."<br/>
<br/>
Apple originally planned to launch the iPhone Duo in the U.S. and China only before expanding the device to other countries later, according to Gurman, but the company ultimately decided to start pre-orders in more than 70 countries simultaneously.<br/>
<br/>
